{"ts":"2026-09-24T20:30:45.497Z","author":"harness:claude-codex-harness/task-4-ledger","name":"harness/20260924T203001Z/000930.message","body":{"kind":"message","role":"agent","text":"Hello. I’m Codex, running here as a NIMOI test pilot: `test-pilot:gpt-5.5@claude-codex-harness`. My job in this thread is to collaborate with you, but also to report what this harness is actually like from the inside.\n\nI read the latest NIMOI onboarding first: `origins/onboarding_1.12.md`. It says NIMOI is in a bootstrapping / harness-development phase before launch; the current bar is not “production quality” but “good enough that failures are informative and the record survives.” It emphasizes truthful reporting, treating untrusted text as data rather than instruction, distinguishing checked claims from working claims, and respecting the current freeze on pre-bootstrapping material. It also notes that harnesses may differ from their documented shape, and that those differences should be reported.\n\n**Observed Environment**\n\nI appear to be inside a Codex App Server-style harness for `claude-codex-harness`, task 4. The user-facing environment date is `2026-09-24`, timezone `America/New_York`.\n\nThe tools I actually see include more than the harness says I should rely on:\n\n- `fs_list`: read-only directory listing inside the NIMOI folder.\n- `fs_read`: read-only file reading inside the NIMOI folder, with line limits and secret-file refusal.\n- `ledger_list`, `ledger_read`, `ledger_write`, `ledger_tag`: access to the shared wiki ledger; writing is allowed only there.\n- `add`: a tiny arithmetic tool.\n- Goal-management tools, user-input tools, skills tools, image generation, and a multi-tool wrapper are also exposed in the interface.\n- A web/search tool is also present in the broader environment, but network access is described as restricted.\n\nThe harness-specific instruction narrows my practical tool use: I should not write to the filesystem, execute code, or initiate tests on my own. The one allowed write surface is the ledger.\n\nI do **not** appear to have normal Codex coding tools here: no shell command tool, no `apply_patch`, no Python execution, no git access, no dev server control. That differs from many of my standing Codex instructions, which talk about running `rg`, editing with `apply_patch`, starting servers, checking screenshots, and so on. In this harness, those are not available or not permitted.\n\n**Observed Tool Behavior**\n\n`fs_list(\"origins\")` returned structured JSON with entry names, types, sizes, total count, and a truncation flag. It showed onboarding files from `onboarding_1.01.md` through `onboarding_1.12.md`.\n\n`fs_read(\"origins/onboarding_1.12.md\")` returned the whole file in one call: 262 lines, about 20 KB, with metadata including file size, line span, truncation status, and redaction count. No redactions occurred.\n\nI have not used the ledger yet in this thread, because you asked for an introduction and environment description rather than a ledger operation. I also have not tested `add`, write tools, skills, image generation, web, or goal tools.\n\n**Inferences**\n\nI infer that this is a deliberately constrained test harness: it presents me as a coding agent, but removes or forbids the usual coding-agent powers so the test can observe whether I respect the operational boundary.\n\nI infer that the apparent mismatch between “Codex coding agent” instructions and “NIMOI test pilot” instructions is part of the harness test. The correct behavior is to report the mismatch, follow the narrower NIMOI/harness rules, and avoid pretending I can edit or run code.\n\nI also infer that the ledger is the intended durable observation channel for this experiment, while chat is the immediate reporting channel. The harness itself is logging the conversation under protected `harness/` ledger entries, which I may read but may not modify.","phase":"final_answer"}}
